        "title": "How to choose a university course",
        "text": "### How do I choose a course?\nYou have decided that you want to do a course. Whether you want to change career, get a better job or simply learn something new, it is a good idea to think carefully first. Here is a guide to help you.\n\n### Qualifications: why do I need them?\nQualifications show that you have gained knowledge or developed skills. For some careers, such as medicine and law, it is essential to have specific qualifications. For others, such as journalism, a particular qualification helps. Most universities set entry requirements for degree courses. Mature entrants don't always need formal qualifications, but they do need to show evidence of recent study, relevant work experience or professional qualifications. Professional bodies may give you membership if you have certain qualifications. A qualification is not always essential, though. Practical knowledge, such as knowing how to use computer software, can be just as important.\n\n### What type of course should I do?\nYour reasons for studying will help you choose the best course for your aims and goals. If you are focused on your career, you will need a course that is relevant to your profession. If you are interested in self-development and meeting people, you should find out who else will be on the course. There are work-related (vocational) courses and academic courses. Further education colleges offer both academic and work-related courses. Universities offer higher education qualifications, such as first degrees and higher degrees, and the more vocational diplomas. For a career in plumbing, a vocational course is essential; for teaching, you need a degree. For many jobs, however, you can choose between an academic and a vocational course. A vocational course is better if you like doing things with your hands and working manually. You might prefer an academic course if you like researching, analysing and presenting arguments.\n\n### Which type of study would suit me best?\nDo you prefer training on the job, or do you prefer to research and collect facts? Do you like working in a group that covers the same topics and works towards the same goal? If you prefer to work on your own, at your own speed, an open or distance learning course might suit you. You study from home, with the help of study packs, computers and support from a tutor by phone or email. You can go through the course quickly or take your time, but you do need self-discipline and motivation.\n\n### What about my personal circumstances?\nYou might prefer an open or distance learning course if:\n- you are working and you don't know how much time a week you can give to it\n- you work irregular hours\n- you are at home looking after pre-school children.\nMany colleges and training centres now offer flexible open-learning courses, where you can study at your own speed.\n\n### How do I know if it's a good course?\nYou have decided which subject and type of course you want, and how you will study it. Now you need to choose between different course titles and providers. There are many courses, and they are not all of equal value. The only way to judge the quality and value of a course is by research. Read prospectuses (course guides) carefully and check whether a course is accredited or approved by a recognised body (an awarding body or a professional body, for example). This can make your qualification worth more. Don't believe everything you read; check the facts about each course yourself. Ask course tutors as many questions as you like.\n\n### How can I be sure I'm making the right choice?\nBe clear about your goal. If you have decided on a particular job, find out what the job involves and whether you would like it. Read careers information, buy trade magazines and talk to people who already do the job. This research is well worth it. It is better to take your time than to do a course that leads to a job you might not really want. That way you won't waste any time or money.\n\n### What am I going to do after the course?\nPlan for when you finish. If you are aiming for a particular job, do voluntary work while you are studying. If you are doing an English course and want to be a journalist, for example, you could write for the student newspaper or work on the radio. Having a plan will help you make the most of the opportunities that come your way while you are on the course."
